
                  Directory Opus external string file format

                        v1.0 (c) 1992 Jonathan Potter


The Directory Opus external string file is a file of strings that can be
modified to change the text strings used in Directory Opus. This is used
primarily to support non-English languages, but could also be used by
the user to further customise the program.

The string file is an IFF file of form OSTR (Opus STRings).
It consists of one header chunk (STHD - STring HeaDer), followed by
a number of string chunks (STRN).

These structures are defined as follows :

        /* String header chunk - STHD */

        typedef struct {
            ULONG version;      /* Version of this string file */
            ULONG stringcount;  /* Number of strings defined */
            ULONG flags;        /* Flags, set to 0 for now */
            char  language[20]; /* The name of this language */
        } StringHeader;


        /* String chunk - STRN */

        typedef struct {
            ULONG string_id;    /* Reference number of this string */
            char string[];      /* The replacement string, null-terminated */
        } DOpusString;


The detailed meaning of the fields of these structures are:

        StringHeader.version

        This is the version number of this string file. The version number
        is used by the program reading the string file to determine whether
        the string file is too old to be usable. Due to the nature of the
        string file format, strings can be added and deleted without
        requiring a version number change. However, any change to
        existing strings should be accompanied by a version number change.

        StringHeader.stringcount

        This gives the number of replacement strings defined by this
        file (the number of STRN chunks present).

        StringHeader.flags

        Flags field for future expansion, should be 0 for now.

        StringHeader.language

        Allows you to give a name to this string file; typically this
        will be the name of the language that these strings represent.


        DOpusString.string_id

        This is a reference number used by the program reading the
        string file, and is used to identify each string.

        DopusString.string

        This is the actual replacement string. It may be any length,
        and must be null-terminated.


A typical SIFTed string file fragment might look like this :

        . F0RM 10010 D0ST
        . . STHD 32 D0ST
        . . STRN 65 D0ST
        . . STRN 83 D0ST
        . . STRN 34 D0ST
        . . STRN 67 D0ST
        . . STRN 36 D0ST
        . . STRN 26 D0ST


Note that you do not need to specify all possible strings, nor do they
have to be in any particular order.
